WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.200
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.983
So far, you've only been drawing gray colored shapes.

00:00:09.983 --> 00:00:12.983
Let's throw some color into these shapes to make them more interesting.

00:00:13.703 --> 00:00:19.967
Every shape has two elements that can be colored, a stroke and a fill. The stroke is the border.

00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:24.567
of the shape. The fill is the color inside the shape. At any point you can

00:00:24.567 --> 00:00:28.927
change the color used to draw the stroke or fill of the shape. For example,

00:00:28.927 --> 00:00:29.951
here we'll change the color to a little bit.

00:00:29.951 --> 00:00:35.551
the fill to be the color green before drawing a rectangle. If you draw more rectangles after

00:00:35.551 --> 00:00:39.591
setting the fill color, they'll also be green. Until you change the fill color, you'll be

00:00:39.591 --> 00:00:39.591
ready to do that.

00:00:39.935 --> 00:00:43.935
color, GameLab will continue to draw shapes in the color that you set.

00:00:43.935 --> 00:00:45.855
The same applies for the stroke of your shapes.

00:00:49.919 --> 00:00:57.119
If you don't want your shapes to have a stroke or a fill, you can use the "no stroke" or "no fill" commands to make it so.

00:00:57.839 --> 00:00:59.903
The order of commands matters a lot in game.

00:00:59.903 --> 00:01:03.903
You need to change the color of your stroke or fill before you draw the shape.

00:01:03.903 --> 00:01:09.887
If you were drawing or painting in real life, this would be like dipping your paintbrush into your palette before drawing on the canvas.

00:01:09.887 --> 00:01:15.127
the canvas. There's lots of other commands you can use to create interesting and complex

00:01:15.127 --> 00:01:19.487
drawings. As you get more familiar with all the drawing tools available in GameLab, you'll

00:01:19.487 --> 00:01:19.871
get better

00:01:19.871 --> 00:01:31.711
bringing your ideas to life. Happy drawing!
